+\subsection{Joint inference across the six score contrasts}\label{sec:joint}
+Table~\ref{tab:scores} and Table~\ref{tab:intervals} report six primary score contrasts, two per asset. Read one at a time, three of them exclude zero. That reading ignores two things: the six statistics are computed on the same 2,766 sessions for three highly correlated funds, and separate 95\% intervals do not control the probability of at least one false exclusion.
+
+We therefore test the six contrasts jointly. The daily paired loss differences are aligned on the common target sessions and resampled with the same moving-block indices in every draw, so that dependence across assets and across contrasts is preserved. Observed statistics are standardized by the bootstrap standard deviation, and adjusted p-values follow the Romano--Wolf stepdown: the maximum statistic is first screened over all six hypotheses, and then over the hypotheses not yet rejected. A single-step max-$|t|$ critical value supplies simultaneous intervals; because it controls the family in one step it is more conservative than the stepdown test, and the two procedures need not agree at the boundary.
+
+Table~\ref{tab:joint} gives the result. Only the QQQ unrestricted-minus-IID contrast is decisive (stepdown-adjusted $p=0.008$, simultaneous interval $[-0.0210,-0.0024]$). IWM sits exactly on the boundary: its stepdown p-value is 0.048, just below 5\%, while its single-step simultaneous interval still contains zero and Holm's procedure on the same raw p-values gives 0.056. We therefore report IWM as unresolved rather than rejected. SPY is not rejected either (0.093 and 0.097). No reversible-minus-unrestricted contrast approaches significance; the smallest adjusted p-value is 0.19.
+
+The primary conclusion is unaffected: under every multiplicity convention, detailed balance shows no advantage. The secondary conclusion does change. The statement that the unrestricted chain has the lowest log loss for every asset is true of the point estimates and false as a familywise statement: it is a QQQ result, with IWM unresolved at the boundary and SPY not distinguishable.
 
